Declare failures that callers can handle. Keep defects distinct from those declared outcomes.
Catch a failure where the application can recover or report it truthfully.

## Give expected failures a type and useful context

`Schema.TaggedError` defines a yieldable error with a tag and declared fields.
`FileStatsError` keeps the path and the filesystem reason.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/stats.ts at=65e9465f38092e24486392f22c08f45d61230c20 lines=13-26
```

Inside a generator, `return yield*` can raise a yieldable error directly.
`Effect.fail` can also place an error in the expected failure channel.
Do not throw an ordinary exception to represent a declared refusal.

## Translate implementation failures without losing their meaning

The inspector uses `Effect.mapError` to translate a filesystem failure into its domain error.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/file-inspector.ts at=65e9465f38092e24486392f22c08f45d61230c20 lines=20-26
```

Translation keeps the failed path and the source message. Callers receive `FileStatsError`, not a filesystem dependency.

If a domain error needs to carry an unknown source error, `Schema.Defect()` can describe that field.
A field using that schema does not itself turn an expected error into a runtime defect.

## Recover or report at a named boundary

`Effect.catchTag` handles the selected failure tag. Other failures remain failed.
Use `catchTags` when several tagged failures need different handlers.

The CLI reports the inspection failure, then fails again:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=apps/cli/src/cli.ts at=65e9465f38092e24486392f22c08f45d61230c20 lines=13-15
```

A fallback is appropriate only when its value is a valid domain result.
Do not turn a failed inspection into empty file statistics just to complete the command.

## Do not use orDie to hide ordinary failures

`Effect.orDie` removes expected failures from the typed channel by turning them into defects.
The inspection machine uses it at the actor boundary because its expected file failure already travels through the `Unreadable` outcome.
An unexpected actor failure or missing outcome is a programming failure at that boundary.

This is a specific boundary decision. File reads still return `FileStatsError` to ordinary callers.
Use `Effect.catchDefect` only where an integration boundary must inspect unexpected failures.
Do not treat it as general domain recovery.

## Common wrong approach

Catching every cause and returning success erases whether the program refused work or crashed.
A later caller cannot tell which repair is needed.
Keep refusal, unexpected failure, and successful completion distinct.

## Effect idiom and house rule

- Expected failures appear in the Effect type. Tagged handlers can recover without swallowing defects.
- In rat-stack, errors retain source context and repair guidance. Finite lifecycles use XState through the pinned Effect bridge.
- The application chooses which lifecycles need machines and which diagnostic fields each error needs.

See also: [schemas](/lore/schemas-define-the-boundary), [author-facing diagnostics](/lore/errors-are-part-of-the-interface), [independent failures](/lore/accumulate-independent-errors), and [lifecycles](/lore/lifecycles-are-machines).
